      "text": "To determine how many ways there are to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, we need to find all pairs \\((n, m)\\) such that \\( n \\times m = 86400 \\). This involves factoring 86400 into all its positive integer factors and finding pairs \\((n, m)\\) where both \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) are positive integers.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorization of 86400:\n\\[\n86400 = 864 \\times 100\n\\]\n\\[\n864 = 2^5 \\times 3^3\n\\]\n\\[\n100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\\[\n86400 = 2^7 \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we list all the factors of 86400. We do this by considering all combinations of the prime factors raised to their possible powers. For each factor \\( n \\) of 86400, \\( m \\) will be \\( \\frac{86400}{n} \\). We will list all the factors of 86400:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, 15, 16, 18, 20, 24, 27, 30, 32, 36, 40, 45, 48, 54, 60, 72, 80, 90, 96, 108, 120, 144, 180, 216, 240, 320, 360, 432, 540, 640, 720, 1080, 1296, 2160, 4320, 8640, 17280, 21600, 43200, 86400\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWe need to count the number of these factors. Notice that each factor \\( n \\) is paired with a corresponding factor \\( m = \\frac{86400}{n} \\). Therefore, each factor \\( n \\) (where \\( n \\) is counted once) corresponds to a unique factor \\( m \\). Since the factor pairs \\((n, m)\\) are distinct, we have 86400 unique factor pairs.\n\nThus, the number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{86400}\n\\]",
