      "text": "To determine the number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, we need to find the number of solutions to the equation\n\n\\[\nn \\cdot m = 86400\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) are positive integers. This equation is essentially finding the number of divisors of 86400. First, we need to find the prime factorization of 86400.\n\nStarting with the smallest prime, 2:\n\\[\n86400 \\div 2 = 43200\n\\]\n\\[\n43200 \\div 2 = 21600\n\\]\n\\[\n21600 \\div 2 = 10800\n\\]\n\\[\n10800 \\div 2 = 5400\n\\]\n\\[\n5400 \\div 2 = 2700\n\\]\n\\[\n2700 \\div 2 = 1350\n\\]\n\\[\n1350 \\div 2 = 675\n\\]\n675 is divisible by 3:\n\\[\n675 \\div 3 = 225\n\\]\n\\[\n225 \\div 3 = 75\n\\]\n\\[\n75 \\div 3 = 25\n\\]\n25 is divisible by 5:\n\\[\n25 \\div 5 = 5\n\\]\n\\[\n5 \\div 5 = 1\n\\]\n\nSo, the prime factorization of 86400 is:\n\\[\n86400 = 2^7 \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nTo find the number of divisors of 86400, we use the formula for the number of divisors derived from the prime factorization. If a number \\( n \\) has a prime factorization of the form \\( p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\), then the number of divisors of \\( n \\) is given by:\n\\[\n(e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1)\n\\]\n\nFor \\( 86400 = 2^7 \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2 \\), the number of divisors is:\n\\[\n(7 + 1)(3 + 1)(2 + 1) = 8 \\times 4 \\times 3 = 96\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds is \\(\\boxed{96}\\).",
